<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Alavid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">864 CE – 928 CE · capital Amol</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Zaydi Shia state on the Caspian coast whose imams converted the Daylamites to Islam and opened the way for Daylamite power across Iran.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 864 the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruyan">Ruyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, angered by land seizures under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirid</a> governors, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd">Hasan ibn Zayd</a>, a descendant of the Prophet's grandson Hasan, to lead them. With Daylamite support he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> and established a Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, calling himself al-Da'i ila'l-Haqq, 'the summoner to the truth'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Alavids ruled in the name of a religious mission rather than the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> with the help of Daylamite and Gilite warriors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd">Hasan ibn Zayd</a> was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-zayd">Muhammad ibn Zayd</a>, killed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> in 900. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-utrush">Hasan al-Utrush</a>, known as al-Nasir al-Kabir, restored the state in 914 and converted many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilaks">Gilites</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaydism">Zaydi Islam</a>, writing works of law still respected by Zaydis.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-utrush">al-Utrush</a>'s death in 917 the dynasty became a tool of Daylamite commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makan-ibn-kaki">Makan ibn Kaki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a>, and it ended around 928. Its converts went on to found the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/alavid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Alavid dynasty" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: محک · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>864 CE – 928 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amol</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasan ibn Zayd, Muhammad ibn Zayd, Hasan al-Utrush</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zaydi Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Tabari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alavids, Zaydi state of Tabaristan, Alids of Tabaristan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=896&amp;focus=alavid-dynasty" aria-label="Alavid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5996 862 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alavid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6316 1118L6480 1127M6316 1118L6588 1010"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6480 1127h0M6588 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6316 1118h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6316 1118h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amol. <a href="/atlas?year=896&amp;focus=alavid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
